messages:
Alicia: Heads up — AtlasBank POC hit two blockers: 1) Rust SDK fails under their corporate TLS inspection, 2) they want an example of function/tool calling that returns strict JSON for downstream payment processing. They also asked pricing for bursty traffic and our answers to SSO/KMS questions. Can someone pair with me?

Sam: I can jump in. Which environment are they using for the POC? Hosted or Dedicated?

Alicia: Hosted for now. They want to validate integration before discussing Dedicated.

Rishi: Quick note on the Rust issue: native-tls in many Rust crates refuses to accept a proxy-substituted cert chain. Two pragmatic fixes: 1) Instruct their infra to add our egress IPs to the allowlist so MITM isn't applied to traffic to api.redwood.ai; 2) For POC only, we can provide a flag in the SDK to accept the proxy CA (not for production). I can share a small patch to the Rust client that supports explicit CA bundle path.

Rishi: Minimal SSE/websocket example (tokio + tungstenite) we handed to the customer: 

```
use tokio_tungstenite::connect_async;
use futures_util::StreamExt;

async fn stream_example(key: &str) -> Result<(), Box<dyn std::error::Error>> {
  let url = "wss://api.redwood.ai/v1/stream?model=rl-small&format=sse";
  let (mut ws, _) = connect_async(url).await?;
  ws.send(Message::Text(format!("{\\\"prompt\\\":\\\"charge 12.50 to acct 987\\\"} ", ))).await?;
  while let Some(msg) = ws.next().await {
    match msg? {
      Message::Text(t) => println!("chunk: {}", t),
      _ => (),
    }
  }
  Ok(())
}
```

Rishi: For the tool JSON responses, we recommend the func-json schema. Example payload the model returns when it wants to call a payment tool: 

```
{"tool": "init_payment", "args": {"amount": 1250, "currency": "USD", "payee_id": "acct-987"}}
```

Rishi: The client should validate the JSON against a provided schema and respond by calling the tool endpoint securely. We provided a tiny serde struct they can reuse.

Jordan: On the proxy / TLS side: if Atlas can't exempt our IPs they need to install a CA on the host running the Rust service or use our short-lived proxy credentials. For hosted POCs we can spin up a temporary staging edge in a region that avoids their proxy (if policy permits). Also watch out for websocket ping timeouts — their proxy was dropping idle connections after 30s; recommend application-level pings every 15s.

Nina: Security Qs answered briefly: Hosted uses AWS KMS with HSM-backed keys for encryption at rest; we support customer-managed keys for Dedicated/Private (BYOK). We maintain SOC2 Type II and GDPR DPIA docs. For SSO: we support SAML 2.0 and OIDC. Data retention and deletion: logs are retained per our enterprise contract; we can negotiate retention. I'll attach the security pack after this thread.

Kevin: Pricing sketch for their ask (1k TPS bursts, average 150 tokens per request): Hosted burst model estimate ~ $20k-$35k/month depending on burst profile and caching. Dedicated reserved commit for sustained 1k TPS with SLA estimate ~ $60k-$90k/month. We can do a more precise model if they share sample traffic patterns and average token counts.

Alicia: Action items before tomorrow's call:
- Rishi: produce patch/flag for Rust SDK to accept custom CA or guidance for adding CA bundle (P1)
- Jordan: prepare staging edge option + list of egress IPs for allowlist (P1)
- Nina: share SOC2 + SAML/OIDC config doc (P1)
- Kevin: rough cost sheet with two scenarios (hosted burst vs dedicated steady) (P2)

Sam: I'll join the call. Add me to the invite.

ci-bot: Staging namespace `atlasbank-poc-2026` provisioned, creds: `REDS_TEST_KEY_abc` (expires in 24h).

Alicia: Thanks team. I'll also ask Atlas to try a quick curl over their proxy to our staging endpoint and paste the error log. That will tell us if it's TLS or websocket idle timeouts.

Rishi: One more note for the runbook: if you use the func-json tool flow, return a deterministic correlation id in the tool args so the payment system can idempotently apply charges. Example: `{"args": {"amount":1250, "trace_id":"atlas-xyz-2026-03-21"}}`.

Alicia: Perfect. I'll summarize after the call and attach the runbook + sample code. :white_check_mark:
